 **Parameters:**
-*   **Grid Dimensions:** Typically $256 \times 128 \times 256$ in prototype scenes [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Voxel Size:** $0.0625$ units [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Epsilon:** $1.0 \times 10^{-7}$ for floating-point comparisons in boundary checks [S2, unofficial].
+*   `voxel_size`: Typically 1.0 to eliminate division in coordinate mapping [S1, unofficial].
+*   `max_steps`: Optional limit to prevent infinite loops in degenerate cases (e.g., ray parallel to grid face but outside bounds) [S9, unofficial]. Default 128 for a 64x64x64 grid [S9, unofficial].
 
 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Axis-Aligned Rays:** If $dx=0$, $dy=0$, or $dz=0$, the error accumulation logic must handle zero deltas to avoid division by zero or infinite loops. The algorithm effectively degenerates to a 1D or 2D line [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Tied Boundaries:** When multiple axes have the same minimum distance to the next boundary (within epsilon), the standard Bresenham algorithm may visit only one neighbor, potentially missing a voxel that the ray grazes. This is a topological incompleteness issue [S1, S2, S10, unofficial].
-*   **Precision:** Using integer arithmetic avoids floating-point drift, but the error term $c1, c2$ must be initialized correctly to prevent bias [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Axis-Aligned Rays:** If a ray direction component is zero, the standard DDA division by zero is avoided in Bresenham's integer logic. However, the dominant axis selection must handle ties (e.g., if $dx=0, dy=0, dz>0$, Z is dominant) [S3, S8, unofficial].
+*   **Boundary Conditions:** The algorithm must clamp coordinates to $[0, \text{dim}-1]$ to avoid out-of-bounds memory access [S9, unofficial].
+*   **Connectivity:** The standard Bresenham algorithm produces a 6-connected path in 3D (moving along one axis at a time, or diagonally across faces). A 26-connected path (allowing corner crossings) may miss intersections that a 6-connected path detects, but requires less computation [S5, unofficial]. The basic integer accumulation described above yields a path that is topologically consistent with the line segment but may not visit every voxel touched by the continuous ray if the ray passes through a voxel corner [S5, S12, unofficial].
+*   **Precision:** Using integer arithmetic avoids floating-point accumulation errors, but the initial mapping from world to voxel coordinates must be precise [S1, unofficial].
 
